{"retractions" [[17592186327819 :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [17592186327819 :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186327821] "immutable_fields" [[17592186327820 :answer-field/id] [17592186327820 :answer-field/key] [17592186327820 :answer-field/t